</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="establish-mailbox"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Prepare a safe, voluntary test</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Choose a trusted consenting contact or a second account that you own. Confirm the exact From address and the recipient address before sending. Use a neutral subject that is easy to recognise, such as “Email check 15 September”, and a short message with no personal documents, passwords, one-time codes or attachments.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Note the local time and whether you are using webmail or an app. If the provider shows a storage warning, record it before testing. If you cannot access the account or are unsure which address is active, stop and handle account access separately.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A self-send can be useful, but it has limits: the same provider or device may not test every part of an outside delivery route. A second account or consenting contact gives a more informative comparison.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="inspect-folders"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Compare what each mailbox records</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before sending, look at Inbox, Sent, Outbox, Drafts and Junk or Spam. After sending, check whether the message leaves Outbox and appears in Sent. The recipient should check Inbox and relevant filtering folders. Record the times rather than relying only on notifications.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">The Sent folder shows that the app or provider accepted the message for sending. It does not prove that the recipient's provider accepted it, that it reached the Inbox, or that anyone read it. A notification is also not a complete delivery record.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the message appears in webmail but not the app, compare the app's sync state. If it appears in the app but not webmail, check whether the app is showing a local-only copy. Do not remove or rebuild the account to make the displays match.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="send-reply-result"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Send once, reply once, then interpret carefully</h2>
<ol class="mb-6 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li>Send the neutral message once after checking From and To.</li><li>Record the send time and check Sent, Outbox and any bounce or private error.</li><li>Have the recipient reply once, or reply from the second account you control.</li><li>Record when the reply appears in Inbox and whether either side sees a Junk or Spam result.</li></ol><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the outbound message fails in webmail and the app, the provider, account or recipient details may need attention. If webmail works but the app does not, focus on the app's display or sync. If the test succeeds, it only shows that this route worked at that time. It does not prove universal delivery, future delivery or read confirmation.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For app-specific background, see <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6383854?hl=en">Google's Gmail troubleshooting guidance</a> and <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/102578">Apple's Mail guidance</a>. For Outlook.com storage and bounce limits, see <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/outlook/can-t-send-or-receive-email-in-outlook-com">Microsoft's guidance</a>.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep the test private") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Never use real secrets or sensitive attachments in a troubleshooting message. Do not send repeated tests to an unwilling recipient, and do not weaken two-step verification, spam protection or other security controls to force a result.</p>{% endcall %}
